              sentence: "Duplicate sample already received.",
              fills: [])
    ]

    // A label is half of a refusal here — ‘Hold reason is required’ is the sentence somebody reads —
    // so the labels are contract too, and are filled into the text-required refusal rather than
    // concatenated on three platforms.
    static let fieldLabels: [FieldLabel] = [
        .init(id: "cancellation-reason", label: "Cancellation reason"),
        .init(id: "clinician", label: "Clinician"),
        .init(id: "clinical-note", label: "Clinical note"),
        .init(id: "clinical-impression", label: "Clinical impression"),
        .init(id: "supporting-evidence", label: "Supporting evidence"),
        .init(id: "review-rationale", label: "Review rationale"),
        .init(id: "prescription-item", label: "Prescription item"),
        .init(id: "prescriber-directions", label: "Prescriber directions"),
        .init(id: "hold-reason", label: "Hold reason")
    ]

    static let appointmentStates: [AppointmentState] = [
        .init(id: "scheduled", name: "Scheduled",
              detail: "A time and a clinician are held. Nobody has arrived.", terminal: false),
        .init(id: "arrived", name: "Arrived",
              detail: "The visit has started. A consultation may now be opened against it.", terminal: false),
        .init(id: "completed", name: "Completed",
              detail: "The encounter is signed and the visit is closed.", terminal: true),
        .init(id: "cancelled", name: "Cancelled",
              detail: "Called off, with a reason on the record.", terminal: true)
    ]

    // The table was a Record literal in appointments.ts and is now the only copy. Two things it
    // refuses on purpose: an appointment cannot go back to scheduled once somebody has arrived, and
    // neither terminal state has a way out. A visit that can be un-completed is a record that can be
    // rewritten after it was signed, and a cancellation that can be reversed is a cancellation
    // reason nobody has to mean.
    static let appointmentTransitions: [String: [String]] = [
        "scheduled": ["arrived", "cancelled"],
        "arrived": ["completed", "cancelled"],
        "completed": [],
        "cancelled": []
    ]

    // Two, and the refusal sentence for a missing mode names both by id. The generator checks that
    // it still does, so a third mode cannot be added without somebody reading the sentence a patient
    // sees.
    static let visitModes: [VisitMode] = [
        .init(id: "home", name: "At home", detail: "A nurse travels to the address on the visit."),
        .init(id: "video", name: "By video", detail: "A teleconsultation. packages/catalog/teleconsult.json owns what may and may not be done in one.")
    ]

    // The map was a ternary chain in wearables.ts. A sample whose unit does not match its metric is
    // rejected rather than converted, because a kernel that quietly converts a unit is a kernel that
    // will one day quietly convert the wrong one.
    static let metrics: [Metric] = [
        .init(id: "heart-rate", name: "Heart rate", unit: "bpm"),
        .init(id: "steps", name: "Steps", unit: "count")
    ]
    static let sampleQualities: [SampleQuality] = [
        .init(id: "accepted", name: "Accepted", detail: "The device reported a clean reading."),
        .init(id: "poor-contact", name: "Poor contact", detail: "The device reported it was not sitting properly. Stored, and never treated as the latest value.")
    ]
    // One source, and it is labelled a simulator. There is no device in this product: `devices` is a
    // simulated capability, and a sample that did not say where it came from could be mistaken for
    // one that did.
    static let sampleSources: [String] = ["simulator"]
    // No engine reads a sample and concludes anything. A heart rate does not raise an assessment,
    // does not escalate a visit and does not appear in a diagnosis unless a clinician typed it there
    // as evidence under their own name.

    // The four headings are not written down here. They are records.json's, they already appear on
    // the consultation form in all three apps, and a second list would be the copy that drifts.
    // `mustBe` is 4 because two of the refusals below say the word ‘four’ out loud, so the day
    // somebody adds a fifth section the generator fails rather than the sentence quietly becoming a
    // lie.
    static let soapKeys: [String] = ["subjective", "objective", "assessment", "plan"]

    // Three roles, and the honest statement of each is what it may NOT do. Verification itself is
    // not modelled here: `verified` arrives from the host, and packages/catalog/vetting.json owns
    // what being verified means and which checks decide it.
    static let clinicalRoles: [ClinicalRole] = [
        .init(id: "nurse", vettingRole: "nurse",
              may: "Schedule and move a visit, open and write a consultation, propose an assessment, record a wearable connection.",
              mayNot: "Confirm an assessment, sign an encounter, request a medicine or dispense one."),
        .init(id: "doctor", vettingRole: "doctor",
              may: "Everything a nurse may, and confirming an assessment, signing an encounter and requesting a medicine.",
              mayNot: "Verify or release a dispense."),
        .init(id: "pharmacist", vettingRole: "pharmacy",
              may: "Verify a request against the original and the allergy record, hold it with a written reason, release it against a named batch.",
              mayNot: "Write a consultation, propose or confirm an assessment, or request a medicine.")
    ]

    // Every number the kernel enforces, in one place, each with the reason it is that number and not
    // another. Two of the refusal sentences are filled from these values rather than repeating them,
    // so a bound cannot be changed without the sentence changing with it.
    /// minutes
    static let appointmentMinutesMin = 10
    /// minutes
    static let appointmentMinutesMax = 180
    /// characters
    static let noteCharactersMax = 5000
    /// units of a medicine
    static let quantityMin = 1
    /// units of a medicine
    static let quantityMax = 1000
    /// milliseconds
    static let futureToleranceMsMax = 60000
    /// characters
    static let idempotencyKeyCharactersMax = 200
    /// milliseconds
    static let sampleStaleAfterMsMax = 300000
    /// samples
    static let sampleBufferMax = 500
    /// events
    static let auditBufferMax = 1000

    // The record types in packages/catalog/records.json that this kernel writes to. It is a list of
    // ids and not a list of definitions: what each one is, which area it belongs to and who may open
    // it are that file's, and the generator fails if a name here is not one of them.
    static let recordTypes: [String] = ["appointment", "home-visit", "consultation", "diagnosis", "prescription", "dispense", "vitals", "audit"]
    // Named rather than invented. Every one of these is simulated or absent in
    // packages/catalog/capabilities.json, and this kernel is the simulation — a sandbox in memory,
    // seeded with three fictional patients, holding nothing when the tab closes.
    static let capabilities: [String] = ["booking", "clinical-records", "dispensing", "devices"]

    // No engine here interprets a clinical value, scores a patient, ranks a queue, suggests a
    // diagnosis, or lets one person perform two of the three dispensing acts. There is no override,
    // no force flag and no administrative path that skips a check — an operator who needs one of
    // these refusals lifted needs a clinician, not a button.

    static func refusal(_ id: String) -> Refusal? { refusals.first { $0.id == id } }
    static func fieldLabel(_ id: String) -> String? { fieldLabels.first { $0.id == id }?.label }
    static func unit(_ metric: String) -> String? { metrics.first { $0.id == metric }?.unit }
    static func transitions(from state: String) -> [String] { appointmentTransitions[state] ?? [] }

    /// The sentence with its placeholders filled, exactly as the web kernel fills them. A refusal
    /// is looked up first so that a missing id is nil here rather than an empty sentence in front
    /// of somebody being told no.
    static func fill(_ refusal: Refusal, _ fills: [String: String] = [:]) -> String {
        refusal.fills.reduce(refusal.sentence) { text, token in
            text.replacingOccurrences(of: "{\(token)}", with: fills[token] ?? "")
        }
    }

    /// Grouped by hand rather than by locale, so a sentence a patient reads does not change shape
    /// with the device's region setting. Bounds are positive whole numbers — the generator throws
    /// otherwise — so there is no sign to carry.
    static func grouped(_ value: Int) -> String {
        let digits = String(value)
        var out = ""
        for (index, character) in digits.enumerated() {
            if index > 0 && (digits.count - index) % 3 == 0 { out.append(",") }
            out.append(character)
        }
        return out
    }
}
